  "problem": "Express $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-18x+12}{x(x-2)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-18x+12}{x(x-2)^{2}}\\,=$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x$ |  | $x-2$ |  | $(x-2)^{2}$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-18x+12}{x(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-2}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $x(x-2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-18x+12=A(x-2)^{2}+Bx(x-2)+Cx.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-18(0)+12 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0)(0-2)+C(0) \\\\ 12 & =A(-2)^{2} \\\\ 12 & =4A \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2)^{2}-18(2)+12 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2)(2-2)+C(2) \\\\ 4 & =C(2) \\\\ 4 & =2C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=1$ and using our values for $A$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)^{2}-18(1)+12 & =3(1-2)^{2}+B(1)(1-2)+2(1) \\\\ 1 & =3(-1)^{2}+B(-1)+2 \\\\ 1 & =3-B+2 \\\\ 1 & =5-B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-18x+12}{x(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$",
